+
+## 0.9 Hardened Implementation Contract
+
+This section is normative. It supplements the existing Parts I–XXIII above. *(“below” in `0.9.7_Hardened_1`, where this section preceded them.)*
+
+### [IMP-1] Specification-to-implementation traceability
+
+Every normative rule MUST have:
+
+1. a stable rule identifier;
+2. a canonical specification location;
+3. at least one positive or negative conformance test;
+4. a compiler/runtime/toolchain owner;
+5. a diagnostic mapping when violation is diagnosable;
+6. an implementation-completeness state.
+
+No compiler component may treat an unowned rule as optional merely because its implementation has not yet been written.
+
+### [IMP-2] No semantic invention
+
+When the specification is silent on language semantics, the compiler MUST NOT infer semantics from C, C++, Rust, Swift, Python, or another language. It MUST emit an `OWNER DECISION REQUIRED` record and block semantic finalization.
+
+Implementation details that do not affect observable language semantics MAY be selected by the implementation when the choice is the simplest sound and predictable one.
+
+### [IMP-3] Canonical artifacts
+
+The following artifacts are canonical:
+
+| Artifact | Authority |
+|---|---|
+| Language semantics | This specification |
+| Public std API signatures | `std/**/*.em` plus normative library contracts |
+| Compiler IR invariants | Part XIX and this amendment |
+| Runtime ABI | Part XIX plus canonical ABI descriptors |
+| Diagnostics | Error registry + diagnostic rules |
+| Conformance | `tests/conformance/**` |
+| Build/toolchain behavior | Part XX |
+| RageV integration | Part XXII |
+| Owner decisions | `docs/DECISIONS.md` and accepted owner amendments |
+
+Generated artifacts MUST NOT redefine any of the above.
+
+### [IMP-4] Implementation completeness
+
+A feature is not considered implemented merely because source code parses or code generation succeeds. A feature is complete only when its implementation-completeness record has:
+
+```text
+SPECIFIED
+PARSED
+TYPED
+MIR
+VERIFIED
+CODEGEN
+RUNTIME          (when applicable)
+ABI              (when applicable)
+DIAGNOSTICS      (when applicable)
+CONFORMANCE
+DOCUMENTED
+```
+
+All applicable states MUST be complete before the feature can be declared stable.
+
+### [IMP-5] Canonical type identity
+
+The compiler MUST provide one canonical `TypeIdentity` representation for every type.
+
+`TypeIdentity` MUST distinguish, at minimum:
+
+- primitive types;
+- nominal types;
+- generic instantiations;
+- const-generic instantiations;
+- associated types;
+- function and closure types;
+- coroutine frame types;
+- dynamic interface types;
+- `Self`;
+- aliases after canonicalization;
+- foreign types;
+- opaque/generated types.
+
+The same canonical identity MUST be used wherever semantic type identity affects:
+
+- generic solving;
+- monomorphisation;
+- symbol mangling;
+- ABI descriptors;
+- reflection;
+- incremental compilation;
+- build hashing;
+- hot-reload schemas;
+- debugger type identity.
+
+No subsystem MAY invent an incompatible type-name canonicalization.
+
+### [IMP-6] MIR source provenance
+
+Every MIR statement, terminator, compiler-generated safety check, borrow operation, drop operation, and runtime-check emission MUST retain source provenance sufficient to produce an accurate diagnostic.
+
+Optimisation passes MUST preserve provenance or explicitly merge it according to a documented rule.
+
+### [IMP-7] Compiler pass and canonical-fact contracts
+
+Every compiler pass MUST declare:
+
+```text
+input invariants
+output invariants
+semantic preservation obligations
+diagnostic obligations
+source-provenance obligations
+```
+
+The verifier MUST run after every safety-critical MIR transformation.
+
+The 0.9.6 reference compiler MUST exchange shared semantic facts rather than re-solving the same
+relationship independently in each feature. The canonical set is `TypeIdentity`, `BorrowCapability`,
+`OwnershipGraph`, `AccessContract`, `InitializationState`, `LayoutDescriptor`, and `EffectSet`.
+Their concrete Rust representation is implementation-defined, but their semantic boundaries are not.
+
+Each semantic invariant MUST have one authoritative representation. Derived indexes, caches,
+summaries, serialized metadata, and backend-specific forms MAY exist only when they are mechanically
+derived from that authority, checked for consistency, and invalidated whenever an input authority
+changes. A stale derived fact MUST NOT be used to accept a program. A call-level aggregate such as
+`CallInfo { access_contract, effect_set, provenance, retention }` is therefore permitted only as a
+derived cache: it MUST NOT replace, redefine, or become independently authoritative over any member.
+
+For explanation and implementation organization, the facts MAY be grouped as provenance, storage,
+authority, validity, initialization, and effects. Grouping MUST NOT collapse independent facts.
+In particular, `AccessContract` and `EffectSet` remain separate; provenance and storage identity remain
+separate; and `InitializationState` retains `Uninit | Maybe | Init` plus field, element, range, and
+other refinements required by dataflow. Unknown initialization is `Maybe` or not proven initialized;
+it is never silently treated as definitely uninitialized.
+
+A `BorrowCapability` conceptually contains:
+
+```text
+provenance_root
+source_place?                  # absent for static/non-place sources
+storage_identity
+projection_path
+region
+access_permission             # shared | mut
+reference_kind                # reference | raw_pointer | value | view | handle | runtime_guard
+ownership_relation            # owned | borrowed | observing
+acquisition_or_check          # static | runtime_checked | lock_acquired
+safety_authority              # safe | unsafe
+synchronization_domain
+validity_interval
+escape_constraints            # a set/predicate, not one exclusive enum
+```
+
+Lifetime provenance and concrete storage identity MUST remain separate. A static provenance root need
+not have a local source place; distinct Arena allocations may share one Arena region while retaining
+distinct storage identities. Access permission, representation kind, ownership, checking mechanism,
+unsafe authority, and synchronization MUST remain orthogonal: `raw_pointer` is a reference kind, not
+an access permission, and `unsafe` is an authority boundary, not a proof algorithm. Region equality or
+inequality alone MUST NOT establish storage overlap, disjointness, or `noalias`.
+
+`synchronization_domain` distinguishes `none`, `thread_confined`, and `synchronized` without changing
+the ownership relation. `validity_interval` records where use is permitted; it MUST NOT be substituted
+for ownership or storage identity. `escape_constraints` can simultaneously express such existing
+questions as return, storage, capture, FFI publication, statement/block survival, and crossing `yield`.
+Storage identity plus projection may establish disjointness only through an existing proof such as
+distinct allocation identities or non-overlapping Arena ranges, disjoint fields or SoA columns,
+`split_at`-style structure, or a proof-carrying disjointness API.
+
+Feature modules consume these facts while retaining their authoritative semantics. Arena remains a
+region allocator; `Cell`, `RefCell`, synchronization guards, and `UnsafeCell` retain different
+invariants; ECS remains library-owned; FFI remains contract-driven; coroutine suspension does not
+extend a forbidden borrow; and H10's dynamic class-exclusivity mechanism MUST NOT turn an ordinary
+borrow conflict into a runtime-checked acceptance.
+
+Across every domain, absence of proof grants no capability. Unknown borrow overlap supplies no
+disjointness or `noalias` fact; unknown callable access covers all relevant fields; unknown foreign
+retention covers all relevant regions; a soundly bounded dynamic target uses the union of its targets
+and an unbounded target is conservative; and an unsafe validity claim supplies no automatic proof.
+This least-permissive policy MUST preserve compile-time safety guarantees rather than replacing them
+with an unrequested runtime best effort.
+
+Before adding a public type, interface, syntax form, annotation, or feature-specific safety subsystem,
+the design and implementation MUST determine whether an existing generic parameter, interface,
+parameter mode, concrete API, compiler fact, or existing explicit proof path expresses the requirement.
+Inference and existing metadata MUST be attempted before new source syntax. A new abstraction is
+permitted only when the existing mechanisms cannot express the requirement without loss of safety,
+clarity, ABI guarantees, or required performance. The reference compiler MUST NOT create a parallel
+safety authority merely because two feature paths currently use different code.
+
+These canonical fields are compiler facts, not a new public type or ordinary-diagnostic vocabulary.
+The compiler MUST NOT expose a public helper merely because internal analysis paths differ. Normal
+diagnostics name source values, places, and semantic causes; existing expert inspection tooling MAY
+show region, storage, projection, capability, access-summary, and MIR details.
+
+
+### [IMP-8] One consolidated normative source
+
+Within this development target, the current addenda and the consolidated Parts I–XXIII body form
+one normative contract. The front-matter authority boundary determines whether that target has
+been adopted as the repository's normative specification.
+Frozen predecessor files remain historical evidence outside this document. A rule ID MUST have
+exactly one definition in the consolidated source; a later revision replaces wording in place or
+uses a new, previously unused rule ID. Historical change-log prose is descriptive and MUST NOT be
+extracted as a current definition. Rule-index, grammar, version-selection, owner-decision, and
+conformance tools consume this one source directly; no duplicated registry or position-based
+supersession algorithm is a second source of truth.
+
+The normative specification, implementation ledger, owner-decision ledger, and conformance evidence
+are separate artifacts with separate jobs. None may silently override another: the specification
+defines required behavior, the implementation ledger records current support, the owner ledger records
+authority for changes, and executable conformance records evidence. An implementation document SHOULD
+map an existing rule to architecture rather than restating that rule as a second semantic contract.
+
+### [IMP-9] Canonical standard-library definition ownership
+
+A public standard-library symbol MUST have exactly one defining module. Re-exports MUST be explicitly marked as re-exports and MUST NOT create a second canonical definition. For interior mutability, `std.cell` is the defining owner of `Cell`, `RefCell`, `Ref`, and `RefMut`; prelude exposure is re-export only.
+
