+## 0.9.6_Hardened_6 Span API Completion Record
+
+**Version treatment:** owner-approved standard-library API and safety-boundary completion of a
+surface already advertised by the inherited Span contract. **Amendment class:** OWNER-APPROVED
+SEMANTIC COMPLETION. H5 remains the immutable predecessor. This cut changes only the previously
+unresolved identity and behavior of the named iterator, chunk, and raw-pointer operations; it does
+not introduce another ownership, lifetime, iterator, opaque-return, raw-pointer, or unsafe model.
+
+**Source authority.** The owner-supplied ODR-014 ruling is preserved byte-for-byte at
+`docs/spec-source/as-received/ODR-014_Span_MutSpan_API_completion.md` (SHA-256
+`0B2BC1BCB6450F266EC9EC264035C2F6C4A469574591CD8C9BDC8B9421A7058F`). Its `*const T` spelling is
+transport notation from a C/Rust-style type vocabulary; current Ember has always spelled a shared
+raw pointer `*T` and a mutable raw pointer `*mut T`. H6 applies that existing spelling without
+altering the owner's selected const/mutable authority boundary.
+
+**Resolved contract.** `std.collections` publicly exports the non-prelude named `@view` types
+`SpanIter`, `MutSpanIter`, `SpanChunks`, and `MutSpanChunks`. Shared iteration yields `ref T`;
+mutable iteration yields `ref mut T`; shared chunks yield `Span[T]`; mutable chunks yield disjoint
+`MutSpan[T]`. Mutable iterator/chunk construction reborrows rather than consumes its `MutSpan`, and
+ordinary NLL governs later reuse. A zero chunk size panics through the existing failure path in every
+profile. `as_ptr` and `as_mut_ptr` are safe extraction operations returning `*T` and `*mut T`;
+dereference, access, arithmetic, reference construction, and validity obligations remain governed by
+the existing unsafe rules. No raw pointer extends or retains source storage lifetime.
+
+**Evidence boundary.** H6 specifies the target and `[TST-25]` matrix; it does not claim that the
+compiler, runtime, standard library, or conformance suite already implements that matrix. The
+adopted repository specification remains `docs/spec-source/ember-spec.md`
+(`0.8.5_Hardened_1`) until the explicit adoption gates and owner action install a successor.
+
+## 0.9.6_Hardened_5 Simplicity-Consolidation Adoption Record
+
+**Version treatment:** owner-approved architecture, tooling, documentation, and development-contract
+hardening. **Amendment class:** REFERENCE-IMPLEMENTATION, TOOLCHAIN, DOCUMENTATION, and PROCESS;
+SEMANTIC CHANGE: NONE. H4 remains the immutable predecessor. The received RFC is preserved
+byte-for-byte as
+`docs/spec-source/as-received/Ember_0.9.6_Simplicity_RFC_Revision_5.md` (SHA-256
+`0AB0F9C2F4F52E10492A0CD94897BBD9C8B47C08D86E7246EA2FA29F18007525`).
+
+**Owner clarification.** The RFC's original statements that adoption alone would not create H5 were
+procedural safeguards against an unreviewed document silently becoming normative. The owner has now
+explicitly approved materialization as H5. That later ruling supersedes only the RFC's no-version-bump
+statements; it does not convert the RFC into an implementation-status ledger or authorize any source
+semantic change.
+
+**Adopted contract.** Ember keeps the smallest practical programmer-visible vocabulary while allowing
+sophisticated compiler internals. Each semantic invariant has one authoritative representation.
+Indexes, caches, summaries, and `CallInfo`-style aggregates are derived, invalidated with their source
+facts, and never independently trusted. Absence of proof grants no capability. Existing generic,
+interface, parameter-mode, metadata, and concrete APIs are considered before a new public abstraction;
+inference is attempted before new annotations. Initialization retains `Uninit | Maybe | Init` and all
+required finer-grained facts. `AccessContract`, `EffectSet`, provenance, retention, storage identity,
+permission, representation, ownership, checking mechanism, unsafe authority, synchronization,
+validity, escape constraints, and initialization remain independent where H4 requires them.
+
+**Surface result.** This hardening adds no `Len` or `Capacity` interface, no parallel safety system,
+no new runtime capability representation, and no automatic dynamic-borrow fallback. Existing
+collections reuse existing interfaces only when their semantics match. Programmer documentation and
+primary diagnostics explain source-level ownership, permission, provenance, escape, effects, and
+allocation rather than exposing compiler bookkeeping as a second language vocabulary.
+
+**Evidence boundary.** H5 is a target contract, not evidence that the compiler has completed the
+consolidation. Existing behavior, specified-but-unimplemented behavior, and conformance evidence remain
+separate ledgers. Adoption of H5 as `docs/spec-source/ember-spec.md` still requires all recorded gates.
+
+## 0.9.6_Hardened_4 Static Hasher Boundary Completion Record
+
+**Version treatment:** owner-approved hardening of the public hashing protocol completed in H3.
+**Amendment class:** OWNER-APPROVED SEMANTIC COMPLETION. H3 remains the immutable predecessor. This
+cut changes only the previously unresolved callable/interface representation of `Hash.hash`; it does
+not change the remaining hashing, equality, collection, ownership, borrowing, ABI, effect, reload,
+or determinism contracts.
+
+**Resolved contract.** `Hash.hash` is generic over `H: Hasher`. The concrete argument type is
+inferred and normally monomorphized through the existing generic/interface machinery. Bare `Hasher`
+is not treated as a concrete parameter type, and no implicit `dyn` conversion is introduced.
+`DefaultHasher` implements `Hasher`. Runtime-polymorphic hashing remains available only to a future
+API that explicitly specifies a legal `dyn Hasher` representation.
+
+**Evidence boundary.** This specification completion does not claim compiler or conformance support
+for the public hashing surface. Executable status remains external repository evidence.
+
+## 0.9.6_Hardened_3 Hashing Contract Completion Record
+
+**Version treatment:** owner-approved standard-library API completion of a protocol already required
+by the existing Map contracts. **Amendment class:** OWNER-APPROVED SEMANTIC COMPLETION. H2 remains
+the immutable predecessor. This cut does not change existing equality, collection ownership,
+borrowing, Arena allocation, failure, effect, FFI, ABI, reload, or determinism semantics.
+
+**Resolved contract.** `std.collections` publicly exports the `Hash` and `Hasher` interfaces and the
+concrete `DefaultHasher`. Existing `[MOD-5]` keeps `Hash` in the prelude; neither `Hasher` nor
+`DefaultHasher` is a prelude name. `Hasher` is stateful and move-only, `finish` consumes it, supplied
+byte spans cannot be retained, and `Hash` implementations must satisfy `Eq` coherence. `Map` and
+`ArenaMap` require `K: Eq + Hash`; their safe APIs expose keys only through shared references. The
+default mixing algorithm is deliberately not a source-compatibility contract.
+
+**Evidence boundary.** This specification completion does not claim that custom-key hashing or the
+complete `DefaultHasher` implementation is present. Executable status remains external repository
+evidence.
